SIGNAL = r"""{\sffamily\bfseries\small\color{Deep}A PROSPERO-registered meta-analysis of 32 wildfire studies (25 pooled, $>$394 million deaths) puts all-cause mortality at RR 1.066 (1.014--1.121) per 10\,$\mu$g/m$^3$ of wildfire-attributed PM$_{2.5}$ and cardiovascular mortality at 1.020 (1.010--1.030); respiratory (1.112, 0.953--1.298) and cancer mortality stayed imprecise.}\\[1.4mm]
\footnotesize
Australian National University (Tong group), \textit{Environmental Research}; random effects, Navigation Guide grading, subgroups by WHO region, lag, income, age and sex. Europe gave the largest regional all-cause estimate (1.045, 1.024--1.066).
\textbf{Where it is weak:} the pooled all-cause RR sits above the largest regional all-cause estimate quoted (Europe), which implies heterogeneity it does not report, and ``wildfire-related PM$_{2.5}$'' is produced by different attribution methods (chemical transport, satellite, statistical separation) across the pooled studies.
\textbf{Why it matters here:} smoke-specific PM$_{2.5}$ is almost always a modelled quantity. Ground networks that resolve smoke episodes at neighbourhood scale are the direct way to shrink the exposure term that dominates this uncertainty."""

